From: hjviewer <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 17:39:45 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 37/59] hjviewer: minimum version: the signed version policy
 and its trust rules

An app pinned to the hjviewer server learns from a signed policy which
version of each platform is the minimum and which the latest, and stops
connecting out when it is below the minimum (minimum version design 1, 3,
6). Whom it believes and what a new policy may change are the trust rules
of the version policy, which replace design 1-4 and which the release
tool's shared vectors pin (hjviewer repository, tools/hjv-release/tests/
vectors, made from their model). This commit is the core in Rust; the
screens, the PC's refusal of old controllers and the Windows installs come
on top of it. Three one-line hooks in shared files (lib.rs: the module;
flutter_ffi.rs: main_get_common and main_set_common, each right after the
account module's hook), everything else in src/hjviewer_update:

- policy.rs: the wire format. The envelope {"v","k","p","s","root"}, the
  policy text and the root declaration, with every field's rule: versions as
  x.y.z without leading zeros and the last number below 100 (the only form on
  which get_version_number is the order of versions), times as
  YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ with a year from 0001 to 9999, package size up to
  200 MiB, lower-case SHA-256, file names and source tags of 1 to 100 plain
  characters that the Windows packer does not act on (the release tool and
  hjv-api read them more strictly, which is theirs alone), text lengths. An
  entry's grace_min is there exactly when grace_until is, and below min:
  before the deadline only versions below grace_min are blocked. A
  declaration has an epoch from 2 on, its issued time, a revoked list that
  may be missing or null, and a base: the four requirement members of each
  of its entries, read as the policy of the declaration's epoch with seq 0.
  The envelope is UTF-8 before anything else is read, and every struct of
  these texts is a JSON object. A field this version does not know is
  skipped, a known field that breaks its rule refuses the whole policy, a
  key given twice or a number that is not a plain integer is no JSON here.
- trust.rs: whom the app believes. The build pins one or two signing keys
  (HJV_UPDATE_PUBKEY), one or two root keys (HJV_UPDATE_ROOT_PUBKEY) and
  the policy position it was made under (HJV_POLICY_FLOOR); a weak or
  invalid key, a key number or key twice, or a root key that is also a
  signing key makes the set unusable, and its fingerprint (SHA-256 of
  "hjv-update-keys-v1", the server, the signing keys and the root keys)
  names it in the log. Signatures are checked over "hjv-version-v1" NUL p
  and "hjv-version-root-v1" NUL root.p, before anything of the text is read.
  The state is the held declaration and the held policy; what the app
  enforces (EFF: of the two signed parts, the later one), compares with
  (BASIS), may install from (MAY_INSTALL, with the floor) and holds
  sequence numbers against follow from them. An envelope goes A, B, C: a
  declaration (a root key's, for this server, of an epoch from the app's on,
  never revoking a root key; another text for the epoch held is a fork) is
  taken first and kept whatever becomes of the policy beside it; a policy
  of epoch 2 or later travels with its declaration, moves the sequence
  number at most 1000, is issued no earlier than its basis, and asks no
  less than it at any time, entry by entry and for the Android pair a PC
  uses together. No policy has special rights for its signer: only a
  declaration asks less. The last position is taken again only with the
  same text, nothing below the build's floor is taken, and a new install
  takes the policy its build was made under.
- store.rs: the record v 2 in HJViewer_update.json of the process's config
  folder: the declaration and the envelope of the policy as received, when
  the server last answered and how far its clock was. At every start each
  signed part is checked again under the keys of the build that reads it; a
  part that does not hold is dropped alone, and a record that keeps nothing
  is a fresh start. SAVE writes nothing over a record of a higher rank (the
  declaration's epoch, then the sequence number of a held policy of that
  epoch, then whether one is held), through a new file named with the
  process ID.
- fetch.rs: GET https://<HJV_SERVER>/api/hjv/version through the strict
  client only (never plain HTTP, never an accepted invalid certificate), at
  most 16 KiB, an answer from a redirected address refused; no ETag goes
  out, and a 304 is a failed check. Limits on a clock that only runs
  forward: one request a minute, one every ten seconds and twelve an hour
  for a check somebody waits for, half an hour off after a failure, and the
  thread's own check every six hours +-10%.
- verdict.rs: the entry an app is judged by (windows, ios; android or
  android_play once the screen said where the app was installed from, and
  none until then, so that an app from Google Play is never blocked by the
  APK's minimum), the states unknown, ok, available, grace and required
  (THRESHOLD: before the deadline grace_min, after it min), os_too_old by
  API level, iOS version or Windows major version, and the clock: the
  device's, never before the enforced policy's signed `issued`, and the
  server's Date only where the device runs more than a day ahead of it, so
  that an unsigned header can put a deadline off but never bring one
  forward. For controllers: "Windows", "Android" (the two Android entries
  together) and "... iOS", refused below LIMIT(now), the lowest of what the
  family's entries block at that time, and told the lowest min; other
  platforms are not checked. A refusal that says "version not allowed" is
  replaced by one of two texts of the app's own, without a link, which keep
  the mark that stops the automatic reconnection: R1 while the app must
  update or has not checked in the last five minutes (the refusal starts a
  check), R2 when it has just checked and the policy asks no update of it.
- door.rs: door 1 of hjv-api. A package request by the login (the token and
  the account key's X-HJV-Proof, through the account module's proof_header
  behind a small trait) or by a device key ("hjv-update-v1" NUL id NUL t NUL
  platform NUL version, in X-HJV-Device, X-HJV-Time, X-HJV-Sig), the short
  link request, whose answer counts only as /api/hjv/u/XXXX-XXXX on the
  build's own server, and the download: a stated length above the signed
  size is refused, at most one byte more than the signed size is read, the
  file is new, named by the app, and stays only if its size and SHA-256 are
  the signed ones. The answers to the login's requests reach the account
  module as every account answer does (observe_account_response), which
  learns the server's clock from them; after a 409 `clock` the request goes
  once more, signed by that clock.
- mod.rs: the build variables, the process's state, the worker thread and the
  entry points. What the app enforces, the clock's issued and the controller
  rule come from EFF; downloads and the state's package only under
  MAY_INSTALL; a refusal that kept a declaration is written and enforced
  but is no answer. main_get_common: hjv-update-state (JSON: state,
  channel, versions, grace_min, deadline, texts, package, policy position,
  check times, job) and hjv-min-version-<platform>. main_set_common:
  hjv-update-check ("" or "now"), hjv-update-device (installer, system
  version), hjv-update-link, hjv-update-download (a folder). The main
  window hears changes as the hjv-update event, and the start log names the
  key set.

A build takes part only if it is pinned (HJV_SERVER) and has usable keys.
Any other build asks no server, starts no thread, reads no file and answers
the screens' keys with nothing, as 1.5.1 does. A build with the E2E commands
(feature hjv-e2e) takes part only for hjv.test with keys whose seeds are of
one repeated byte or of 32 consecutive byte values, the test keys of this
project, so it never acts on a real server's policy; there the Linux client
stands in for a Windows app. The app fails open: without a policy it
believes, it blocks nothing.

Not hooked yet, and so reported as unused by a Flutter build until the next
commits: on_refusal and on_login_refusal (client.rs), controller_rule
(connection.rs), start_service_checks and check_now, and the device key's
request (the Windows service).

Tests (cargo test --lib hjviewer_update, no network): fixed signatures and
hashes made with Python's cryptography package from the byte layouts and
checked with OpenSSL, reproduced on libsodium, and the design's research
vector; every byte of a signed envelope changed; other contexts and keys;
unknown and revoked keys, version 2; the JSON rules (null and twice-given
members at every level, numbers out of range, -0, fractions, lone
surrogates in read and in skipped values, arrays for objects), times from
0001 to 9999 and not 0000; the declaration, its base and their rules,
canonical base64, UTF-8 first; a declaration kept by refusals of every
kind, a fork, Issued, the pending raise and the Android pair, the later
part enforced over a forged policy, a later declaration held alone that
brings its time, a revoked signer's policy, the floor's held states,
COVERS against THRESHOLD on every pair; sequence numbers and the step of
1000; LOAD and SAVE part by part, across key sets and between two
processes; the limits and the period on an injected clock; the table of
states, the clock, the platforms; the texts against upstream's
check_if_retry; the requests against the research vector's device signature
and the account module's proof, and a login's requests against the request
proofs that hjv-api's tests check (the account module's vectors, which
hjv-api reads as tests/data/v3-vectors.json) byte for byte; what the account
module hears of an answer and which 409 is `clock`; a redirected answer,
the read limit and the stated length; link answers; downloads cut, padded
and changed; the screens' JSON; which builds take part.
An opt-in test (HJV_UPDATE_VECTORS, ignored by default) runs the release
tool's vectors (hjviewer repository, tools/hjv-release/tests/vectors)
through the same code: every case's outcome and reason code, the state a
refusal leaves, the cases only the tool refuses, then_accept from the state
the case left, and covers.json.

Known limits: SAVE's read, compare and rename are not one atomic step, and
there is no lock: when two processes of one device save at the same moment,
the record of lower rank can land last (a policy of epoch 1 over a
declaration of epoch 2, say). The device then acts as if the later record
had not come yet, until the next check (30 s after a start, then every six
hours) or the other process's next save brings it back; whoever could hand
one process the lower record at that moment could as well keep the later
one from the other. The login's signature (AccountSigner, the account
module's proof_header) is reached by no unit test: it signs only with an
account key the device keeps, which a test process has not got. It fails
closed, as a 401 of hjv-api; a login's download on a test server checks
it.

(Minimum version design, work package C1; trust rules, work package TR.)

